  "id": "Compound::DB01029 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Irbesartan, a member of the Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists class, is consistently linked to the treatment of hypertension through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Irbesartan, as part of this class, is directly associated with the disease hypertension, with each of its subclasses—Losartan, Valsartan, and Telmisartan—also exhibiting a similar therapeutic relationship. This is supported by the shared lowest common ancestors, which indicate a common biological pathway or mechanism, likely involving the modulation of angiotensin II signaling. The integration of these pathways highlights a recurring mechanism of action, where the receptor antagonism of angiotensin II plays a central role in blood pressure regulation. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust and reliable relationship, reinforcing the notion that Irbesartan is a valid candidate for repurposing in the treatment of hypertension.",
